RabbitMQBus.cmnts
=================

Readable pseudo-code of RabbitMQBus.cs. Every step carries a "//" comment that
explains WHY it is done that way, which alternatives were rejected, and what
would break if it were done differently.

Sections follow the order of RabbitMQBus.cs and are headed by the member name.
Search by name; line numbers are not used because they drift.


==============================================================================
THE SHAPE OF THE CLASS
==============================================================================

  RabbitMQBus
    one consumer connection   + one long-lived channel   (receives)
    one publisher connection  + one long-lived channel   (sends)
    three background loops:
      KeepConnected(consumer)       rebuilds the consumer when it dies
      KeepConnected(publisher)      rebuilds the publisher when it dies
      PublishBufferedMessages       drains the in-memory send buffer

  // WHY two connections instead of one:
  //   When the broker runs low on memory or disk it "blocks" connections that
  //   publish. A blocked connection stops being read by the broker, so on a
  //   shared connection our ACKS for received messages would stall too. The
  //   broker would then stop delivering (prefetch is full of unacked
  //   messages), and the app would freeze in both directions. With separate
  //   connections a publishing problem cannot stop consumption.
  //   This is also RabbitMQ's own recommendation.

  // WHY our own reconnect loops instead of RabbitMQ.Client's
  // AutomaticRecoveryEnabled:
  //   1. The library's recovery only works after the FIRST connect succeeded.
  //      If the broker is down when the app starts, the library just throws.
  //      We need a retry loop for startup anyway; once we have one, it can
  //      handle later drops too, and there is only one code path to test.
  //   2. On reconnect we must do more than replay topology: passively check
  //      other apps' exchanges, re-sync the run-time bindings
  //      (appliedBindings), and re-attach our own consumer callback. Two
  //      recovery mechanisms running at once (library + ours) would race and
  //      could create duplicate consumers.
  //   So the library's recovery is switched off (AutomaticRecoveryEnabled =
  //   false, TopologyRecoveryEnabled = false) and ours is the only one.

  // WHY Publish() only buffers:
  //   Callers are UI event handlers and API requests. They must never wait for
  //   the network or fail because the broker is briefly down. Publish puts the
  //   message in memory and returns; a loop sends it when it can.
  //   Trade-off: the buffer is in memory only. If the process exits while
  //   messages are buffered, they are lost (Dispose logs how many).


==============================================================================
FIELDS
==============================================================================

  options, dispatcher, logger         // set once in the constructor, readonly
  instanceId                          // unique per bus object
  queueName                           // computed once, never changes
  routingKeyPrefix                    // "" when not configured, never null,
                                      // so "prefix + key" needs no null checks

  outstandingMessages : ConcurrentQueue
      // Written by app threads (Publish), read by the publish loop. A
      // concurrent queue gives thread safety and FIFO order without a lock.

  registrations : ConcurrentDictionary<wireName, (type)>
      // Read on every delivery (consumer thread), written by subscribe calls
      // (app threads). Concurrent dictionary avoids locking the hot path.

  consumerLock : SemaphoreSlim(1)
      // Guards "the consumer is being opened / closed / its bindings changed".
      // A SemaphoreSlim, not `lock`, because the guarded code awaits network
      // calls, and `lock` cannot be held across an await.

  publisherLock : SemaphoreSlim(1)
      // Same idea for the publisher: opening and closing it never overlap.
      // See OpenPublisherAsync for the shutdown race it prevents.

  requestedBindings : Dictionary<(exchange, key), refCount>  + bindingGate
      // What the APP asked for. Protected by a plain `lock (bindingGate)`
      // because the code inside never awaits; it is just dictionary edits.
      // Kept separate from consumerLock so BindRoutingKey (called from app
      // code, possibly a UI thread) never waits behind a slow reconnect.

  appliedBindings : HashSet<(exchange, key)>
      // What is REALLY on the broker for the current queue. Only changed
      // while consumerLock is held (OpenConsumer and the sync), so it needs no
      // lock of its own.

  consumerConnection, consumerChannel, consumerQueueName
  publisherConnection, publisherChannel
      // null while disconnected. Assigned only after a FULLY successful open
      // (see OpenConsumerAsync), so other code never sees half-built objects.


==============================================================================
CONSTRUCTOR
==============================================================================

  if options is null                                  -> throw
  if BusName/ExchangeName/ClientName/ExchangeType blank -> throw
  if any subscription has no Exchange                 -> throw
  if dispatcher or logger is null                     -> throw
      // WHY validate here: everything after Start() runs on background loops
      // that log and retry forever. A missing ExchangeName found there would
      // be a warning repeated every 5 seconds instead of a crash at startup
      // that points at the config. Fail fast where the caller can see it.
      // WHY logger is required (no "null logger" fallback): a messaging
      // library that fails silently is worse than one that refuses to start.

  instanceId = options.InstanceId or new GUID
      // The legacy adapter (RabbitMQService) passes its own id so the id it
      // reports matches the bus's. Otherwise each bus gets a fresh one.

  if Shared mode and (MaxAttempts < 1 or DeliveryLimit < 1) -> throw
      // 0 attempts would dead-letter everything unread; a delivery limit of 0
      // makes the broker dead-letter on first delivery.

  routingKeyPrefix = options.RoutingKeyPrefix ?? ""

  if Shared:
      queueName = prefix + (SharedQueueName ?? lower("client_bus"))
      // Same name on every copy of the service: that is what makes them share
      // one queue (competing consumers).
      // Prefix included: a debug copy with "dbg." must NOT join the real
      // service's queue, or it would silently take half of the real traffic.
      // No user name or instance id: the queue belongs to the service, not to
      // whoever happens to run it.
  else (PerInstance):
      queueName = lower("client_bus_windowsUser_instanceId")
      // Must be unique per running copy, so every copy gets every event.
      // The user name is there only so a human can tell queues apart in the
      // management UI ("whose queue is this?").
      // No prefix needed: already unique, and the prefix works through the
      // bindings, not the queue name.
      // Lower case: keeps names consistent regardless of how ClientName was
      // typed in config.


==============================================================================
UsesExchange(exchange)
==============================================================================

  return exchange == own exchange or exchange is in Subscriptions
      // Used to reject BindRoutingKey on an exchange this bus never declared
      // or checked, and by the bus router to choose which bus a binding
      // belongs to. Ordinal comparison because broker names are
      // case-sensitive.


==============================================================================
Start()
==============================================================================

  throw if disposed or already started
      // Two sets of loops would fight over the same connection fields.
  started = true
  fire and forget:
      KeepConnected(consumer)
      KeepConnected(publisher)
      PublishBufferedMessages
  return immediately
      // WHY not wait for the broker: the app (a WPF window, an API) should
      // start even if RabbitMQ is down; messaging catches up when the broker
      // comes back. Each loop catches and logs its own errors, so not
      // awaiting the tasks loses nothing.


==============================================================================
Publish(wireName, message, routingKey = null)
==============================================================================

  throw if disposed or wireName blank
  key  = prefix + (routingKey if given, else own ExchangeName)
      // Default key = own exchange name: the legacy routing convention.
      // Subscribers bind once per exchange with that key and receive all of
      // that app's events. (One binding per event type was tried and
      // crashed the broker at ~300k bindings.)
  body = Serialize(message)  NOW, on the caller's thread
      // WHY serialize now and not in the background loop:
      //   - Errors (unsupported types such as tuples) are thrown to the code
      //     that called Publish, where they make sense. In the loop they would
      //     just be a log line and a message stuck at the head of the buffer
      //     forever, blocking everything behind it.
      //   - It snapshots the object. If the caller changes it after Publish,
      //     what was published does not change.
  enqueue PendingMessage(wireName, key, new MessageId, body, CreatedUtc = now)
      // MessageId: lets receivers detect duplicates (delivery is
      // at-least-once, see the publish loop).
      // CreatedUtc: the AMQP timestamp is "when the app published", not "when
      // the buffer got round to it", which may be minutes later after an
      // outage.


==============================================================================
StartReceiving(registration) / StopReceiving(wireName)
==============================================================================

  StartReceiving:
    add registration if the wire name is not registered yet
    if it was added AND the bus is already started AND Shared mode:
        warn "subscribed after start; earlier messages were dropped"
      // WHY warn: deliveries with no registration are acked and dropped
      // (see HandleDeliveryAsync). A durable shared queue can hold messages
      // from before this app started; if they arrive before the subscription
      // exists, they are gone. Per-instance queues are brand new and empty at
      // connect, so nothing can be lost there and no warning is needed.

  StopReceiving:
    if disposed: return quietly
      // Shutdown code unsubscribes in no particular order, sometimes after
      // the bus is gone. Throwing there would only break shutdown.
    remove the registration
      // Later deliveries for this wire name are acked and dropped. The queue
      // binding stays: it is per exchange, shared by all of that app's events.


==============================================================================
BindRoutingKey(exchange, key)
==============================================================================

  throw if disposed
  if exchange given and not UsesExchange(exchange) -> throw
      // Binding needs the exchange to exist and be part of this bus's
      // topology; otherwise it is a config mistake, better reported now.

  lock bindingGate:
      for each pair in GetRoutingKeyBindings(exchange, key):
          requestedBindings[pair] += 1
          if it went from 0 to 1: changed = true
      // WHY reference counts: two subscriptions may ask for the same key.
      // When one unsubscribes, the binding must stay for the other. Counting
      // means the broker is only touched on 0 -> 1 and 1 -> 0.

  if changed: SyncRoutingKeysInBackground()
      // Runs in the background so the caller (possibly a UI thread) never
      // waits for the network. If not connected yet, nothing happens now:
      // OpenConsumerAsync applies requested bindings at connect.
      // WHY "changed" check: avoids a pointless broker round trip when only a
      // count went up.


==============================================================================
UnbindRoutingKey(exchange, key)
==============================================================================

  if disposed: return quietly          // same reason as StopReceiving
  lock bindingGate:
      for each pair:
          if not requested: skip       // unbinding something never bound is
                                       // harmless, so do not throw
          if count > 1: count -= 1     // someone else still needs it
          else: remove it, changed = true
  if changed: SyncRoutingKeysInBackground()

  // While the consumer is disconnected the sync does nothing. The pair stays
  // in appliedBindings but is no longer wanted, so the sync at the next
  // connect unbinds it (see ForgetBindingsTheQueueLost).
  // Still not covered: bindings left on a shared queue by a PREVIOUS run of
  // the process. appliedBindings starts empty at startup, so the bus does not
  // know about them; they need removing by hand.


==============================================================================
Dispose()
==============================================================================

  if disposed: return                  // safe to call twice
  disposed = true
  if never started:
      dispose the cancellation source; return
      // No loops and no connections exist, nothing else to clean up.

  cancel the token
      // Stops all three loops and aborts in-flight waits and network calls.
      // Handlers in progress see shutdown and leave their message unacked so
      // it is redelivered (see DispatchWithRetriesAsync).
  if send buffer not empty: warn with the count
      // The buffer is memory-only; this makes the loss visible in the logs.
  run on a pool thread, wait at most 5 s:
      CloseConsumer, then ClosePublisher
      // Consumer first: stop taking new messages before anything else.
      // Closing the consumer channel hands unacked messages back to the
      // broker for redelivery.
      // WHY Task.Run(...).Wait instead of await: IDisposable.Dispose is
      // synchronous. Blocking directly on async code from a WPF UI thread can
      // deadlock (the continuation wants the UI thread that is blocked);
      // running it on a pool thread avoids that.
      // WHY a 5 s cap: a dead broker or network must not hang app shutdown.
  on error: warn, do not throw         // shutdown must not crash the app
  dispose the cancellation source


==============================================================================
KeepConnectedAsync(role, isConnected, close, open, token)
==============================================================================

  loop until cancelled:
      try:
          if not isConnected():
              close()
              // WHY close before open: "not connected" includes half-dead
              // states, e.g. the connection is open but its channel was closed
              // by a broker error. Closing first means we never leak a
              // connection and never end up with two.
              open(token)
      catch cancelled during shutdown: exit loop
      catch anything else: warn "could not connect, retrying"
          // The loop must never die. A wrong password, a broker restart or a
          // missing exchange are all "try again later".
      wait ReconnectDelay (default 5 s); exit if cancelled

  // WHY poll every few seconds instead of reacting to the "connection lost"
  // event: one simple loop covers the first connect, a dropped connection, a
  // channel killed by an error, and a failed reconnect. Event-driven
  // recovery needs extra code for each of those and for events arriving
  // while a reconnect is already running. The cost is up to ReconnectDelay of
  // extra downtime, which is acceptable here.


==============================================================================
ConnectAsync(role, token)
==============================================================================

  connection = factory.CreateConnection(name = "Client [Bus] role - user")
      // The name shows in the management UI's connection list. All apps log
      // in with the same broker account, so the name is the only way to tell
      // them apart there.
  on connection shutdown:
      if the broker or network closed it (not us): warn
      // Only logs. Reconnecting is KeepConnectedAsync's job, so there is one
      // place that reconnects. Shutdowns we asked for are not warnings.
  return connection


==============================================================================
OpenConsumerAsync(token)
==============================================================================

  take consumerLock
      // Nothing else may touch the consumer, or appliedBindings, while it is
      // being built.
  try:
      connection = Connect("Consumer")
      channel    = connection.CreateChannel()

      declare OWN exchange (durable, configured type)
          // WHY here as well as in the publisher: the queue is about to be
          // bound to it. If the consumer connects before the publisher, the
          // exchange might not exist yet, and binding to a missing exchange
          // fails with 404. Declare is idempotent, so doing it twice is free.

      for each subscribed exchange (other apps'):
          PASSIVE declare ("does it exist?")   -> throw if 404
          // WHY never create other apps' exchanges: only the owner knows the
          // correct type and durability. If we created "AppA" as topic and
          // AppA later declared it as direct, AppA's own startup would fail
          // with 406. So we only check.
          // WHY fail the whole connect instead of skipping that exchange: a
          // half-subscribed app looks healthy but silently misses events.
          // Failing makes KeepConnected retry every few seconds and log why,
          // until the other app has started once and created its exchange.

      set prefetch = PrefetchCount (per consumer)
          // Without a limit the broker pushes the whole backlog into memory at
          // once. With a limit, a slow app gets work at the pace it acks.

      declare the queue (Shared or PerInstance, see below)

      for each (exchange, key) in ExchangeNameBindings:
          bind queue to exchange with key
          // The configured, always-on bindings: own exchange with own name,
          // each subscribed exchange with its name, all with the prefix.
          // Done directly here (not via the sync) because they are fixed by
          // config and must exist before consuming starts.

      ForgetBindingsTheQueueLost()
      SyncRoutingKeys(connection, queue)
          // Re-apply the run-time bindings the app asked for, and remove the
          // ones it stopped wanting while disconnected.
          // ForgetBindingsTheQueueLost decides what "applied" should mean for
          // the queue we just declared:
          //   PerInstance: forget everything. The old exclusive queue died
          //     with the old connection, taking its bindings with it; this is
          //     a NEW, empty queue.
          //   Shared: the durable queue survived with all its bindings.
          //     - Forget the pairs that are still WANTED, so the sync binds
          //       them again. Bind is idempotent, so this is harmless when the
          //       binding is there, and repairs it when someone deleted and
          //       recreated the queue meanwhile.
          //     - KEEP the pairs that are applied but no longer wanted (the
          //       app unbound them while disconnected). The sync sees
          //       "applied, not wanted" and unbinds them. Clearing everything
          //       here, as the code once did, forgot them and left them on the
          //       broker for good.

      consumer = new AsyncEventingBasicConsumer(channel)
      consumer.Received += OnMessageReceivedAsync
      channel.Consume(queue, autoAck = false, consumerTag = connection name)
          // autoAck false: the broker keeps each message until WE ack it after
          // handling. With autoAck true the broker deletes it on send, and a
          // crash mid-handling would lose it.
          // consumerTag = connection name: readable in the queue's consumer
          // list in the management UI.

      consumerConnection, consumerChannel, consumerQueueName = the new ones
          // Assigned LAST, only after everything succeeded. Until this line
          // IsConsumerConnected stays false, so nothing else uses a
          // half-built consumer, and a failure leaves the fields as "nothing".
      log "Consumer connected"
  on any error:
      close the half-built channel/connection quietly
      rethrow                          // KeepConnected logs it and retries
  finally:
      release consumerLock


==============================================================================
DeclarePerInstanceQueueAsync
==============================================================================

  declare queueName: durable = false, exclusive = true, autoDelete = true
      // A desktop app's queue is temporary by nature:
      //   exclusive  - only this connection may use it; the broker deletes it
      //                when the connection closes. A crashed or killed app
      //                leaves no orphan queue collecting messages forever.
      //   autoDelete - also removed when the consumer goes away.
      //   durable    - pointless for a queue that dies with the connection.
      // Consequence (accepted): events sent while the app is closed are not
      // received. Desktop apps only care about "now".


==============================================================================
DeclareSharedQueueAsync
==============================================================================

  declare "<queue>.dead-letter" as a durable quorum queue      FIRST
      // It must exist before anything can be dead-lettered into it; a message
      // dead-lettered to a missing queue is simply dropped.

  declare "<queue>" as a durable quorum queue with:
      x-delivery-limit          = DeliveryLimit (default 5)
          // CRASH GUARD. Normal failures never reach this: the handler is
          // retried in-process and then rejected to the DLQ. But if the PROCESS
          // dies while handling (crash, kill, deploy), the message is
          // redelivered. A poison message that crashes the process every time
          // would loop forever and take every instance down with it. The
          // broker counts deliveries and dead-letters it after the limit.
          // This is why the queue is quorum: classic queues have no delivery
          // counter.
      x-dead-letter-exchange    = ""  (the default exchange)
      x-dead-letter-routing-key = "<queue>.dead-letter"
          // The default exchange delivers to the queue NAMED like the routing
          // key. So no extra dead-letter exchange has to be declared, owned,
          // bound or cleaned up. Two arguments and it works.
      x-dead-letter-strategy    = at-least-once
          // Without it, dead-lettering is fire-and-forget inside the broker
          // and a message can be lost on the way to the DLQ. With it, the
          // broker keeps the message until the DLQ confirms it.
      x-overflow                = reject-publish
          // Required by the broker for at-least-once dead-lettering (the
          // default "drop oldest" overflow would discard messages the strategy
          // promises to keep).


==============================================================================
DeclareDurableQueueAsync(name, arguments)
==============================================================================

  declare durable, not exclusive, not auto-delete
      // Permanent and shared by every copy of the service.
  on 406 (exists with different settings):
      throw "Queue 'X' already exists with different settings (broker text)."
      // The broker's raw error is a channel shutdown that is hard to read.
      // Happens when someone created the queue by hand as a classic queue, or
      // DeliveryLimit changed. The broker never changes an existing queue's
      // arguments, so a human must delete it; the message names which one.


==============================================================================
CloseConsumerAsync()
==============================================================================

  take consumerLock
      copy connection/channel to locals
      set fields to null
  release consumerLock
  close the locals quietly
      // WHY swap under the lock and close outside: the lock is held only for
      // the instant it takes to swap. From that instant everyone sees "not
      // connected". The slow part (network close, possibly timing out on a
      // dead broker) does not block a waiting sync or reopen.


==============================================================================
OnMessageReceivedAsync(sender, delivery)
==============================================================================

  channel = the channel of the consumer that raised the event
      // NOT the consumerChannel field: a reconnect may have replaced the
      // field already. A delivery tag only means something on the channel the
      // message arrived on; acking it on another channel is a protocol error
      // that closes that channel.
  try:
      settlement = HandleDelivery(delivery)
  catch anything:
      log error; settlement = FailedSettlement
      // Last-resort net. Every delivery must end in exactly one decision.
      // If an exception escaped into RabbitMQ.Client, the message would stay
      // unacked, occupying a prefetch slot until the channel closes.
  Settle(channel, delivery.tag, settlement)


==============================================================================
HandleDeliveryAsync(delivery)
==============================================================================

  headers = DecodeHeaders(delivery.headers)

  if PerInstance and header "source-id" == our instanceId:
      return Ack (ignore)
      // WHY the echo: an app binds its OWN exchange with its own name as key
      // and publishes with that same key, so it receives its own events.
      // Desktop apps react to other apps' events; reacting to their own would
      // mean double handling (the app already did the work before publishing).
      // WHY not in Shared mode: all copies read ONE queue and each message goes
      // to only one copy. A message published by copy X may be handed to X
      // itself; if X dropped it, NO copy would handle it.

  wireName = header "event-type"
             else routing key without the prefix
      // The header is what this library always sends. The routing key
      // fallback is for publishers that do not send the header. The prefix
      // is stripped so a debug run looks up the same names as a normal run.

  if no registration for wireName:
      debug log; return Ack
      // Normal, not an error: the per-exchange binding delivers ALL of
      // another app's events and this app may care about only a few.
      // WHY Ack and not reject/requeue: requeue would redeliver it forever;
      // reject in Shared mode would fill the DLQ with events nobody wanted.

  context = (wireName, bus, messageId, redelivered, headers, exchange, key)
      // Handlers can see where a message came from and whether it is a
      // redelivery (a hint that it might be a duplicate).
  return DispatchWithRetries(registration, context, delivery)


==============================================================================
DispatchWithRetriesAsync(registration, context, delivery)
==============================================================================

  attempts = Shared ? MaxAttempts : 1
      // WHY no retries for desktop: there is no dead-letter queue for a
      // temporary queue, and a UI event is usually stale by the time a retry
      // would succeed. Log and move on.
  body = copy of delivery.body
      // The body memory belongs to RabbitMQ.Client and may be reused once the
      // delivery is finished; owning a copy keeps every retry safe.

  loop attempt = 1, 2, ...:
      message = Deserialize(body, registration.type)
          // A FRESH object per attempt: if a handler changed the object and
          // then threw, the next attempt still gets the original data.
      try:
          dispatcher.Dispatch(message, context)
          return Ack
      catch (only if attempt < attempts and not shutting down):
          warn "attempt n of N failed; retrying"
          // On the LAST attempt this catch does not match (exception filter),
          // so the exception falls through to the outer catches below.
      wait RetryDelay; if cancelled -> return None

  // WHY retry in-process instead of rejecting with requeue = true:
  //   requeue puts the message straight back and it is redelivered at once,
  //   a tight loop with no delay; and each requeue counts against the
  //   delivery limit, so the crash guard would fire on ordinary failures.
  //   In-process retries give a real delay and a clean count.
  //   Cost: while a message waits for its retry, this consumer's slot is busy,
  //   and later messages wait behind it.

  outer catch, if shutting down:
      info log; return None
      // The handler did not really fail; we interrupted it. Leaving it unacked
      // makes the broker redeliver it (to another copy, or to us next time)
      // instead of dead-lettering a perfectly good message.
  outer catch, anything else (all attempts used):
      error log saying what happens next; return FailedSettlement
      // Shared: DeadLetter, kept for a human. PerInstance: Ack, dropped.
      // The log says which, so nobody has to guess where the message went.


==============================================================================
SettleAsync(channel, tag, settlement)
==============================================================================

  if None: return                       // leave unacked -> redelivered later
  if channel closed: return
      // The tag belongs to a dead channel; the broker already took the message
      // back for redelivery. Acking would just throw.
  if DeadLetter: reject(tag, requeue = false)
      // requeue = false is what sends it to the dead-letter target instead of
      // back to the head of the queue.
  else: ack(tag, multiple = false)
      // multiple = false: ack only this message. "multiple" would also ack
      // every earlier unacked message on the channel, including ones other
      // handlers are still working on.
  on error: warn only
      // Worst case the message is redelivered and handled again; that is the
      // normal at-least-once risk, not worth crashing over.


==============================================================================
OpenPublisherAsync(token)
==============================================================================

  take publisherLock (with the bus token)
  connection = Connect("Publisher")
  channel = CreateChannel(confirms = on, confirm tracking = on)
      // Confirms: the broker answers each publish with ack/nack.
      // Tracking: RabbitMQ.Client then makes BasicPublishAsync WAIT for that
      // answer and throw on nack. So "publish returned normally" means "the
      // broker has it", which is exactly what the publish loop needs to decide
      // whether a message may leave the buffer. Without confirms a message
      // written to a socket that is just dying is silently lost.
  declare own exchange
      // Publishing to a missing exchange makes the broker close the channel
      // (404). Declaring first guarantees it exists, even on a fresh broker.
  publisherConnection, publisherChannel = the new ones     (last, on success)
  on error: close quietly, rethrow (KeepConnected retries)
  finally: release publisherLock

  // WHY the lock: Dispose closes the publisher from another thread while the
  // reconnect loop may be in the middle of opening one. Without the lock,
  // Dispose could close (nothing), and a moment later the opener stores a
  // brand-new connection that nobody ever closes. With the lock:
  //   - Open in progress -> Dispose's close waits for it, then closes what it
  //     stored.
  //   - Open starting after Dispose cancelled the token -> WaitAsync(token)
  //     throws at once, so no connection is ever created.
  // The publish loop still reads publisherChannel without the lock: it takes
  // a local copy and only needs "is it open right now".


==============================================================================
ClosePublisherAsync()
==============================================================================

  under publisherLock: copy fields to locals, null the fields
  close the locals quietly (outside the lock)
      // Same "swap under the lock, close outside it" idea as
      // CloseConsumerAsync.


==============================================================================
PublishBufferedMessagesAsync(token)
==============================================================================

  loop until cancelled:
      channel = publisherChannel           // read ONCE into a local
          // The reconnect loop may null or replace the field at any moment.
          // A local copy cannot change between the "is it open?" check and
          // the publish call.
      if channel open and buffer has a message:
          message = PEEK (look, do not remove)
          PublishMessage(channel, message)   // waits for the broker's confirm
          DEQUEUE                            // remove only after the confirm
          continue immediately               // drain the backlog fast
          // WHY peek-then-dequeue: if publishing throws (connection drop,
          // nack), the message is still first in line and is retried. Nothing
          // is lost to a network blip, and ORDER is kept, because nothing
          // behind it can overtake it.
          // Accepted side effect: if the broker stored the message but its
          // confirm was lost with the connection, the retry sends it again.
          // Receivers may see a duplicate (same MessageId). That is the price
          // of never losing one ("at least once").
          // WHY one message at a time: simplest way to keep strict order and
          // exact retry. Throughput is limited to one broker round trip per
          // message, which is far more than these apps publish.
      on error (not shutdown):
          warn with wire name and id; message stays buffered
      wait OutstandingPollInterval (default 100 ms); exit if cancelled
          // Only reached when idle, disconnected, or after an error. A short
          // poll is simpler than a signal between Publish and the loop, and
          // 100 ms of latency is fine for these events.


==============================================================================
PublishMessageAsync(channel, message)
==============================================================================

  headers:
      event-type = wireName      // how receivers pick the .NET type; the
                                 // routing key cannot be used for that because
                                 // it is the app's name, shared by all events
      source-id  = instanceId    // lets a per-instance receiver drop its echo
  properties:
      ContentType = application/json
      MessageId   = the id made in Publish      // duplicate detection
      AppId       = ClientName                  // who sent it, in the UI
      Timestamp   = CreatedUtc in Unix seconds  // AMQP timestamps are seconds
  basicPublish(exchange = OWN exchange, key = message.key, mandatory = false)
      // Own exchange only: in this design an app publishes to its own
      // exchange and others bind to it. Publishers never need to know who
      // listens.
      // mandatory = false: if nobody is bound for this key, the broker drops
      // the message quietly. In pub/sub "nobody listening right now" is
      // normal; mandatory = true would return every such message and need a
      // handler for returns that would only log noise.


==============================================================================
CreateConnectionFactory()
==============================================================================

  host, port, vhost, user, password, heartbeat from options
      // Heartbeat: without it a silently dead network (e.g. VPN drop) is only
      // noticed after the OS TCP timeout, possibly many minutes. With 30 s
      // heartbeats both sides notice within about a minute and reconnect.
  TLS enabled = UseTls; ServerName = TlsServerName or HostName
      // The certificate is checked against ServerName. It defaults to the
      // host, but must be overridable for connecting by IP or by alias.
  AutomaticRecoveryEnabled = false, TopologyRecoveryEnabled = false
      // See "THE SHAPE OF THE CLASS": our loop is the only recovery.
  ClientProperties: app, bus, user, machine, process_id, instance_id
      // Shown on a connection's page in the management UI. With one shared
      // broker account for everyone, this is how you find "which machine and
      // process owns this connection" when something misbehaves.


==============================================================================
GetConnectionName(role) / ProcessId
==============================================================================

  "ClientName [BusName] role - WindowsUser"
      // Readable in the connection list; also used as the consumer tag.

  ProcessId = Process.GetCurrentProcess().Id, disposing the Process object
      // Environment.ProcessId would be shorter but does not exist on
      // net472 / netstandard2.0, which this project also targets.
      // The Process object holds an OS handle, hence the `using`.


==============================================================================
DeclareExchangeAsync / EnsureExchangeExistsAsync
==============================================================================

  DeclareExchange: own exchange, configured type, durable, not auto-delete
      // durable: survives broker restarts, so subscribers' bindings survive.
      // not auto-delete: other apps' bindings must not disappear just because
      // this app is not running.

  EnsureExchangeExists(other): passive declare; 404 -> "Exchange 'X' does not
  exist."
      // See OpenConsumerAsync for why other apps' exchanges are only checked.
      // The short message replaces the broker's channel-shutdown text.


==============================================================================
SubscribedExchanges / ExchangeNameBindings
==============================================================================

  SubscribedExchanges = subscription exchanges, minus our own, distinct
      // Someone may list their own exchange as a subscription, or list one
      // twice. Our own exchange is declared, not passively checked, so it
      // is excluded here.

  ExchangeNameBindings:
      (own exchange,  prefix + own name)
      (each subscription exchange, prefix + its name)
      // One binding per exchange, key = app name (the legacy convention).
      // Receives everything that app publishes; filtering by event happens in
      // HandleDeliveryAsync via registrations. Per-event bindings were tried
      // and overloaded the broker at scale.
      // Own exchange included: needed so the app receives its own events in
      // Shared mode (other copies of the service), and harmless in PerInstance
      // mode thanks to the echo filter.


==============================================================================
WithoutPrefix(key)
==============================================================================

  if key starts with the prefix: strip it, else return key unchanged
      // Only used for the wire-name fallback. Keys that do not start with the
      // prefix are returned unchanged rather than rejected, so a misrouted
      // message still gets its normal "unknown wire name" handling.


==============================================================================
GetRoutingKeyBindings(exchange, key)
==============================================================================

  if key blank -> throw
  exchanges = [exchange] if given, else [own] + subscribed
      // "No exchange" means "this key, wherever I listen". Convenient for
      // custom keys that several apps might publish.
  return each exchange paired with (prefix + key)
      // The prefix is applied HERE, once, so the reference counts, the sync
      // and the broker all use the same final key.


==============================================================================
SyncRoutingKeysInBackground()
==============================================================================

  if not started or disposed: return
      // Before Start there is no consumer; OpenConsumerAsync will sync at
      // connect.
  on a pool thread:
      take consumerLock
          // So a reconnect cannot swap the connection or clear appliedBindings
          // in the middle of a sync.
      if consumer connected: SyncRoutingKeys(consumerConnection, queue)
      release consumerLock
      ignore cancellation / disposed     // shutting down; nothing to report
      warn on anything else              // next connect re-syncs anyway


==============================================================================
SyncRoutingKeysAsync(connection, queue)
==============================================================================

  wanted     = copy of requestedBindings keys   (under bindingGate)
      // A snapshot, so app code can keep calling Bind/Unbind while the slow
      // broker calls run, without "collection modified" errors.
  configured = ExchangeNameBindings

  for each pair in wanted, not configured, not applied:   bind;   if ok add to applied
  for each pair in applied, not wanted:                   unbind; if ok remove from applied

  // WHY compare "wanted" with "applied" instead of replaying each Bind/Unbind
  // call: the result is right no matter how many calls happened, in which
  // order, or whether a reconnect happened in between. Ten bind/unbind calls
  // while disconnected become one comparison at connect.
  // WHY skip configured pairs: those are owned by OpenConsumerAsync. If an app
  // bound and then unbound a custom key that equals a configured one, the
  // sync must not remove the configured binding and silently cut off a
  // subscription.
  // WHY only "if ok": a failed bind is not recorded as applied, so the next
  // sync or connect tries again.
  // .ToList() before each loop: the loop changes appliedBindings, and a set
  // may not be modified while it is being enumerated.


==============================================================================
BindOnBrokerAsync / UnbindOnBrokerAsync / ChangeOnBrokerAsync
==============================================================================

  Bind/Unbind just pass QueueBind or QueueUnbind into ChangeOnBroker.

  ChangeOnBroker(connection, pair, bind, change):
      channel = connection.CreateChannel()     // NEW, short-lived
      change(channel)
      log info "Bound/Unbound key on exchange"; return true
      on 404 while unbinding: info; return true
          // The exchange was deleted, which removes its bindings too. The
          // goal ("not bound") is already met.
      on 404 while binding: warn; return false
          // The exchange does not exist (yet). Not recorded as applied, so it
          // is tried again at the next sync or reconnect.
      on other errors (not cancellation): warn; return false
      finally: close the channel quietly

  WHY A NEW CHANNEL, and not the consumer or publisher channel:

    1. A failed command kills its channel. In AMQP, an error such as 404
       (exchange missing) or 403 (no permission) is a channel-level error:
       the broker closes the channel the command ran on. If that were the
       consumer channel:
         - consumption would stop until the reconnect loop noticed (up to
           ReconnectDelay),
         - every unacked message on it would be handed back and redelivered,
           including ones currently being handled (duplicate work),
         - the handlers' acks for those messages would fail.
       All of that because one optional binding failed. A throw-away channel
       confines the damage to itself.

    2. Channels must not be used by two threads at once. The consumer
       channel is busy with deliveries and acks on the consumer's thread; the
       sync runs on a pool thread. Interleaving a bind with an ack on the same
       channel is not safe in RabbitMQ.Client. A private channel has no
       sharing at all.

    3. The publisher channel is the wrong owner too: it has confirm tracking
       and its own loop, and a binding error there would also kill
       publishing.

    4. It is cheap. A channel is a lightweight slot inside an existing
       connection, opened in one round trip. Binding changes are rare
       (subscribe/unsubscribe), so the cost does not matter.

  WHY THE CONSUMER CONNECTION, and not a new connection:
    A per-instance queue is EXCLUSIVE: only the connection that declared it
    may touch it. Binding it from any other connection fails with 405
    RESOURCE_LOCKED. So the channel must live on the consumer connection, and
    a new connection would also be far more expensive than a channel.


==============================================================================
SMALL HELPERS
==============================================================================

  ThrowIfDisposed
      // Public methods that start work refuse to run after Dispose; the
      // "stop" methods (StopReceiving, UnbindRoutingKey) return quietly
      // instead, see their sections.

  IsOpen(connection, channel) = both non-null and both open
      // A connection can be fine while its channel was killed by an error;
      // checking only the connection would leave the bus "connected" but
      // deaf. Checking both makes KeepConnected rebuild it.

  DecodeHeaders
      // AMQP delivers string headers as raw bytes, so byte[] is decoded as
      // UTF-8; other value types are turned into strings. Always returns a
      // dictionary (never null) so callers need no null checks.

  TryDelayAsync(delay) -> false when cancelled
      // Lets every loop write "if (!await TryDelay(...)) break;" instead of a
      // try/catch around each wait.

  CloseQuietlyAsync(channel, connection)
      // Close channel, then connection, swallowing every error. Used on
      // things that are usually already broken; an error closing them tells
      // us nothing and must not hide the original failure. Channel first
      // because it lives inside the connection.


==============================================================================
BEHAVIOUR THAT IS NOT VISIBLE IN ANY ONE METHOD
==============================================================================

These follow from how the pieces above combine. They are the usual answers to
"why did X happen?" questions.

1. Messages are handled ONE AT A TIME per bus.
     The consumer channel is created without ConsumerDispatchConcurrency, so
     RabbitMQ.Client uses its default of 1: OnMessageReceivedAsync is never
     running twice at once on the same bus.
     // Upside: handlers need no locking against each other, and messages from
     // one queue are handled in arrival order.
     // Downside: one slow handler delays every message behind it. Prefetch
     // (50) only decides how many messages wait in the app's memory; it does
     // NOT make them run in parallel.
     // Shared mode: during retries (MaxAttempts x RetryDelay, default
     // 3 x 1 s) the bus receives nothing else.
     // Shared mode with several copies of a service: order is only kept
     // within one copy. Across copies, message 2 can finish before message 1.

2. Delivery is AT LEAST ONCE, in both directions. Duplicates happen when:
     - the publisher's confirm was lost with the connection, and the publish
       loop sends the message again (same MessageId);
     - the consumer connection dropped (or the app shut down) after a handler
       finished but before the ack reached the broker, so the broker
       redelivers it (Redelivered = true);
     - the process crashed mid-handling (Shared mode; the delivery limit caps
       how often).
     // So handlers that change data should be idempotent: running them twice
     // with the same MessageId must be harmless.

3. The send buffer has NO size limit.
     If the broker is down for a long time, every Publish adds to memory
     (see OutstandingCount). Nothing is ever dropped while the process lives,
     but a busy publisher during a long outage keeps growing, and everything
     buffered is lost if the process exits.

4. A message that cannot be DESERIALIZED is not retried.
     Deserialize runs inside the attempt loop but OUTSIDE the inner try, so a
     bad payload (wrong JSON shape for the registered type) goes straight to
     the outer catch: Shared -> dead-letter queue at once, PerInstance ->
     dropped. Retrying could never fix bad data, so this is the right result,
     but it means the error log says "failed" after attempt 1 of N.

5. Shared mode + different app versions = silently lost messages.
     All copies of a service read one queue. During a rolling deploy, an OLD
     copy without the registration for a new event receives some of those
     events, finds no registration, and ACKS them (HandleDeliveryAsync).
     They are gone, not dead-lettered. Deploy new subscriptions to all copies
     before anything publishes the new event.

6. Registrations: first one wins, silently.
     StartReceiving uses TryAdd. A second registration for the same wire name
     (for example with a different .NET type) is ignored without an error.

7. One missing subscribed exchange blocks ALL receiving on that bus.
     OpenConsumerAsync passively checks every subscribed exchange before it
     consumes. If one is missing, the whole consumer open fails and is
     retried every ReconnectDelay. Until the other app has started once,
     this bus receives nothing at all, not even events from exchanges that
     do exist. Publishing is not affected (separate connection).

8. Config mismatches retry forever rather than stopping.
     Wrong password, wrong vhost, own exchange already existing with another
     type (406), shared queue existing with other arguments (406): all are
     thrown inside the reconnect loop, so the app keeps running and logs the
     same warning every ReconnectDelay. Watch the logs at startup.

9. Self-healing after someone deletes things in the management UI.
     - Own exchange deleted: the next publish fails with 404, the broker
       closes the publisher channel, IsOpen turns false, KeepConnected
       reopens and redeclares it. The message stayed buffered and goes out.
     - Queue deleted: the broker cancels the consumer, but the channel may
       stay open, so IsConsumerConnected can still say true and the bus does
       not notice by itself. A reconnect (or restart) recreates the queue.

10. Shutdown and in-flight handlers.
     Dispose cancels the bus token and closes the consumer, waiting 5 s at
     most for everything. A handler that is running is not stopped by force:
     it receives RabbitMQ.Client's per-delivery token (args.CancellationToken),
     not the bus token. If it finishes after the channel closed, SettleAsync
     skips the ack (channel closed) and the broker redelivers the message
     later: one more case of point 2.
     Publish() after Dispose throws. Publish() before Start() is accepted
     and buffered, but nothing is sent until Start() is called.

11. Leftover bindings from an earlier run.
     A shared queue keeps run-time bindings across process restarts. The bus
     only knows what it bound in THIS run, so a key the previous run bound and
     this run never asks for stays bound. Harmless except for extra traffic
     that ends up acked as "unknown wire name"; remove it in the management
     UI.
